EN[73] = "The devil meanwhile had grown seriously tender at Solokha's: he kissed her hand with the grimaces of an assessor before a priest's daughter, clutched his heart, groaned, and said plainly that if she would not agree to satisfy his passion and reward him, as is the custom, he was ready for anything, would throw himself into the water, and send his soul straight to the pit. Solokha was not so cruel; besides, the devil, as is well known, was working on her side. She did like to see a crowd dragging after her and was seldom without company; this evening, however, she had thought to spend alone, because all the distinguished inhabitants of the village had been invited to the deacon's for kutya. But everything went otherwise: the devil had barely put forward his demand when suddenly the voice of the stout Headman was heard. Solokha ran to open the door, and the nimble devil climbed into a sack that was lying there. The Headman, shaking the snow from his cap and drinking a glass of vodka from Solokha's hand, told her that he had not gone to the deacon's because a blizzard had risen; and seeing a light in her cottage, he had turned in with the intention of spending the evening with her. He had hardly said this when there was a knock at the door and the deacon's voice. <<HEAD>>“Hide me somewhere,”<<N>> whispered the Headman. <<HEAD>>“I don't want to meet the deacon now.”<<N>> Solokha thought a long time where to hide such a solid guest; at last she chose the biggest sack of coal; she poured the coal into a tub, and the stout Headman climbed into the sack, with his moustache, his head and his cap."
EN[74] = "The deacon came in, coughing and rubbing his hands, and told her that no one had come to his house, and that he was heartily glad of this chance to spend a little time at her place, and had not been afraid of the blizzard. Then he came closer to her, coughed, smiled, touched her bare, plump arm with his long fingers, and said, with a look that showed both slyness and self-satisfaction: <<DEACON>>“And what is this you have here, magnificent Solokha?”<<N>> And having said this, he jumped back a little."
EN[75] = "<<SOLOKHA>>“What do you mean, what? An arm, Osip Nikiforovich!”<<N>> answered Solokha."
EN[76] = "<<DEACON>>“Hm! An arm! Heh, heh, heh!”<<N>> said the deacon, heartily pleased with his beginning, and walked about the room."
EN[77] = "<<DEACON>>“And what is this you have here, most precious Solokha?”<<N>> he said, with the same look, approaching her again and lightly seizing her neck with his hand, and in the same manner jumping back."
EN[78] = "<<SOLOKHA>>“As if you can't see, Osip Nikiforovich!”<<N>> answered Solokha. <<SOLOKHA>>“A neck, and on the neck a string of beads.”"
EN[79] = "<<DEACON>>“Hm! On the neck a string of beads! Heh, heh, heh!”<<N>> And the deacon again walked about the room, rubbing his hands."
EN[80] = "<<DEACON>>“And what is this you have here, incomparable Solokha?”<<N>> It is not known what the deacon would now have touched with his long fingers, when suddenly a knock came at the door and the voice of the Cossack Chub."
EN[81] = "<<DEACON>>“Ah, my God, an outsider!”<<N>> cried the deacon in fright. <<DEACON>>“What if they find a person of my calling here? It will reach Father Kondrat!”<<N>> But the deacon's fears were of another kind: he was more afraid that his better half would find out, who with her terrible hand had already turned his thick pigtail into the very thinnest. <<DEACON>>“For God's sake, virtuous Solokha,”<<N>> he said, trembling all over, <<DEACON>>“your kindness, as the Gospel of Luke says, chapter thir... thir... They are knocking, by God, knocking! Oh, hide me somewhere!”"
EN[82] = "Solokha poured the coal from another sack into the tub, and the deacon, not too bulky in body, climbed into it and sat right at the bottom, so that another half sack of coal could have been poured on top of him."
EN[83] = "<<CHUB>>“Good evening, Solokha!”<<N>> said Chub, coming into the cottage. <<CHUB>>“Perhaps you did not expect me, eh? True, you did not expect me? Perhaps I have interrupted you...”<<N>> Chub went on, putting on his face a merry and significant look, which gave notice beforehand that his slow head had been working and was preparing to deliver some sharp and clever joke. <<CHUB>>“Perhaps you were amusing yourselves here with someone! Perhaps you have already hidden somebody, eh?”<<N>> And, delighted with this remark of his, Chub laughed, inwardly triumphing that he alone enjoyed Solokha's favour. <<CHUB>>“Well, Solokha, give me some vodka now. I think my throat has frozen from the accursed frost. God sent such a night before Christmas! How it took hold, you hear, Solokha, how it took hold... ugh, how my hands have gone stiff: I can't unbutton my sheepskin! How the blizzard took hold...”"
EN[84] = "<<VAKULA>>“Open!”<<N>> a voice rang out in the street, accompanied by a thump on the door."
EN[85] = "<<CHUB>>“Somebody is knocking?”<<N>> said Chub, stopping short."
EN[86] = "<<VAKULA>>“Open!”<<N>> they shouted, louder than before."
EN[87] = "<<CHUB>>“It is the blacksmith!”<<N>> said Chub, clutching his cap. <<CHUB>>“Listen, Solokha, put me wherever you like; for nothing in the world will I show myself to that accursed brute, may a blister as big as a haystack rise under each of his eyes, the devil's son!”<<N>> Solokha, frightened herself, rushed about like one in a daze and, forgetting herself, signed to Chub to climb into the very sack in which the deacon was already sitting. The poor deacon did not dare even to show his pain by a cough or a groan when the heavy peasant sat almost on his head and placed his boots, frozen from the frost, on both sides of his temples."
EN[88] = "The blacksmith came in without saying a word, without taking off his cap, and almost fell onto the bench. It was plain that he was in a very bad mood. Just as Solokha was shutting the door behind him, someone knocked again. It was the Cossack Svyerbyhuz. This one could no longer be hidden in a sack, because no such sack could be found. He was heavier in body than the Headman himself, and taller than Chub's friend. And so Solokha led him out into the vegetable garden, to hear from him everything he wanted to tell her."
EN[89] = "The blacksmith looked absently into the corners of his cottage, listening from time to time to the songs of the carollers carrying far off; at last he rested his eyes on the sacks. <<VAKULA>>“Why are these sacks lying here? They should have been taken away long ago. Because of this stupid love I have gone quite silly. Tomorrow is a holiday, and all kinds of rubbish are still lying in the cottage. Take them to the forge!”<<N>> With that the blacksmith bent down to the huge sacks, tied them tighter and prepared to heave them onto his shoulders. But it was plain that his thoughts were wandering God knows where, or else he would have heard how Chub hissed when the rope tying the sack twisted the hair on his head, and the stout Headman began to hiccup quite audibly. <<VAKULA>>“Will that wretched Oksana never get out of my mind?”<<N>> said the blacksmith. <<VAKULA>>“I don't want to think of her; yet I keep thinking, and as if on purpose, of her alone. Why is it that a thought forces itself into the head against one's will? What the devil, the sacks seem to have grown heavier than before! Something besides coal must have been put in here. Fool that I am! I forgot that now everything seems heavier to me. Once upon a time I could bend and straighten a copper coin and a horseshoe in one hand; and now I can't lift sacks of coal. Soon I shall be falling over in the wind. No,”<<N>> he cried, after a pause, taking heart, <<VAKULA>>“what sort of woman am I! I won't let anyone laugh at me. Even ten such sacks, I will lift them all.”<<N>> And he briskly heaved onto his shoulders the sacks that two stout men could not have carried. <<VAKULA>>“Take this one too,”<<N>> he went on, lifting the little one, at the bottom of which the devil lay curled up. <<VAKULA>>“I think I put my tools in here.”<<N>> Saying this, he went out of the cottage, whistling a song:"
EN[90] = "<<VAKULA>>“I'll have no bother with a wife.”"
EN[92] = "Songs and shouts rang out ever louder in the streets. The crowds of jostling people were swelled still more by those who came from the neighbouring villages. The lads played pranks and ran wild to their heart's content. Often among the carols one heard some merry song which one of the young Cossacks had just made up on the spot. Then suddenly one of the crowd, instead of a carol, would let fly a shchedrovka and roar at the top of his voice:"
EN[93] = "“Shchedryk, vedryk! Give us a dumpling! A little lump of porridge, a ring of sausage!”"
EN[94] = "Laughter rewarded the joker. Little windows were raised, and the lean hand of an old woman (they alone, with the staid fathers, stayed in the cottages) was thrust out of the window with a sausage in it or a piece of pie. The lads and girls vied in holding out their sacks and catching their booty. In one place the lads, coming up from all sides, surrounded a crowd of girls: noise, shouting, one threw a snowball, another snatched a sack full of all sorts of things. In another place the girls caught a lad, tripped him, and he flew headlong to the ground together with his sack. It seemed they were ready to make merry all night long. And the night, as if on purpose, glowed so luxuriously! and the light of the moon seemed still whiter from the glitter of the snow. The blacksmith stopped with his sacks. In the crowd of girls he fancied he heard Oksana's voice and thin laughter. Every little vein in him trembled; throwing the sacks on the ground, so that the deacon at the bottom groaned from the bump and the Headman hiccuped at the top of his voice, he trudged with the small sack on his shoulders after the crowd of lads who were following the girls' crowd, among whom he had heard Oksana's voice."
EN[95] = "Yes: it is she! She stands like a queen, and her black eyes flash! A fine lad is telling her something; surely something funny, because she is laughing. But she is always laughing. As if involuntarily, not understanding himself how, the blacksmith pushed through the crowd and stood beside her. <<OKSANA>>“Ah, Vakula, you are here! Good evening!”<<N>> said the beauty with the very same smile that nearly drove Vakula out of his mind. <<OKSANA>>“Well, have you collected much? Oh, what a small sack! And the shoes the Empress wears, have you got them? Get me the shoes and I will marry you!”<<N>> And, laughing, she ran off with the crowd."
EN[96] = "The blacksmith stood rooted to the spot. <<VAKULA>>“No, I can't; I have no more strength...”<<N>> he said at last. <<VAKULA>>“But, my God, why is she so devilishly beautiful? Her look, and her words, and everything, it just burns and burns... No, I cannot master myself any longer! It is time to put an end to everything: let my soul perish, I will go and drown myself in the ice-hole, and goodbye!”<<N>> Then with a resolute step he went forward, overtook the crowd, drew level with Oksana, and said in a firm voice: <<VAKULA>>“Farewell, Oksana! Find yourself whatever husband you like, fool whom you like; but you will not see me any more in this world.”<<N>> The beauty seemed surprised, wanted to say something, but the blacksmith waved his hand and ran off."
EN[97] = "<<LADS>>“Where to, Vakula?”<<N>> shouted the lads, seeing the blacksmith run. <<VAKULA>>“Farewell, brothers!”<<N>> shouted the blacksmith in reply. <<VAKULA>>“God willing, we shall meet in the next world; but in this one we shall not make merry together any more. Farewell, do not think ill of me! Tell Father Kondrat to say a requiem for my sinful soul. I have not painted the candles for the icons of the Wonder-Worker and the Mother of God, I am sinful, being busy with worldly affairs. All the goods that are found in my chest, for the church! Farewell!”<<N>> Having said this, the blacksmith began to run again with the sack on his back. <<LADS>>“He has gone out of his mind!”<<N>> said the lads. <<WOMAN>>“A lost soul!”<<N>> an old woman passing by muttered piously. <<WOMAN>>“I will go and tell how the blacksmith hanged himself!”"
